This code finds and collects the driver use handles of "top.f[2:3]" in the loaded design, and
collects the driver signals and relative information into "drvLoadVec".

1. This API supports statement based trace, that is, it gets the driver not only from the
use statement, but also upper statement.

reg a, b, x, y, z;
if( a & b ) /* if statement */
x = y;
if( a | b ) /* if-else statement */
x = ~y;
x = z;
end

To trace driver of "top.x", perform the following steps:
a. Get npiUse from this signal (statements at line 5, 7 and 9).
b. Check target signal is drived of this statement. ("top.y" of line 5 and 7, "top.z" at line
9)
c. Get driver signals of this statement.
&#61623: For assignment and port, need do bit-level trace.
d. If this statement is port/mapElem, pass through.
&#61623: If the argument isPassThrough is true, check the direction and
determine pass or not.
e. Get npiUpperStmt of this statement. (statements at line 4 and 6)
f. Get driver signals of this statement. ("top.a" and "top.b" of both line 4 and 6)
g. Recursive 5~6 steps until the upperStmt is instance.
2. For the npiCondition of npiEventControl, only check the expression whose op code
is posedge or negedge.

reg clk, a, b;
parameter delay = 10;
begin: BLK_INIT
end

always@(b or posedge a)
delay clk = ~clk;

The drivers of "top.clk" are top.clk, top.delay and top.a, but top.b is not.
3. This API does not report npiVhProcess, but it still to get npiUpperStmt.
4. The source signals are the equivalent signals of user input, include its npiRefObj and
npiActual.

interface I;
logic [7:0] r;
int x=1;
bit R;
modport B (input .P(r[3:0]), output x);
module M2 ( interface i, output reg clk, input [31:0] varInt);
always@(*) begin
clk = i.P[2];
i.x = varInt ;
end
reg CLK2;
wire [31:0] input2;
I i1 ();
M2 u2 (i1.B, CLK2, input2);
assign input2[2] = CLK2;

The npiMpPort "top.i1.B.P" and npiRefObj "top.u2.i.P" are equivalent signals.
5. You can specify if the signals in the variable index should be reported or not.
assign f = g[e];

When trcOptions.trcVarIdx = false, the driver signal of "f" is "g[e]" and "e". Otherwise,
the driver signals are "g[e]" and "e".
6. You can specify if the signals of non-edge-trigger event needs to be ignored or not.

always@(aa)
h = bb;

When trcOptions.edgeCheck = true, the driver signal of h is bb. Otherwise, the
driver signals are bband aa.

