The following details the npi_trace_load_by_hdl2 signal:
1. This API supports statement based trace, that is, it gets the load not only from the use
statement, but also child statements.

reg a, b, x, y, z;
if( a & b ) /* if statement */
x = y;
if( a | b ) /* if-else statement */
x = ~y;
x = z;
end

To trace "top.a" load, perform the following steps:
a. Get npiUse from this signal. (Statements at line 4 and 6).
b. Check target signal is loaded of this statement.
c. Get load signals of this statement.
&#61623: For assignment and port, perform bit-level trace.
d. If this statement is port/mapElem, pass through.
&#61623: If the argument isPassThrough is true, check the direction and
determine pass or not.
e. Traverse sub statement of this statement. (Statements at line 5, 7 and 9).
f. Get load signals of this statement. (These three statements are "top.x".)
g. Recursive 5~6 steps until no sub statement.
2. This API does not report npiVhProcess, but it gets the sub statements of this
3. The source signals are the equivalent signals of user input, include its npiRefObj and
npiActual.

interface I;
logic [7:0] r;
int x=1;
bit R;
modport B (input .P(r[3:0]), output x);
module M2 ( interface i, output reg clk, input [31:0] varInt);
always@(*) begin
clk = i.P[2];
i.x = varInt ;
end
reg CLK2;
wire [31:0] input2;
I i1 ();
M2 u2 (i1.B, CLK2, input2);
assign input2[2] = CLK2;

