Specifying the Length of Argument for Attribute-Adding Method
For the attribute-adding methods in NPI FSDB Transaction Writer model, such as
npi_fsdbw_define_attr(), npi_fsdbw_stream_add_attr(), and npi_fsdbw_trans_add_attr(),
users might want to pass a specific range of variable as an argument into these methods
instead of the complete range of a variable.
For example, consider the following pseudo code:
void user_define_function( npiFsdbwTransHandle trans, NPI_INT16 var)
{
truncate_head( var );
npi_fsdbw_add_attr(trans, "truncated_attr", var, npiFsdbwBin);
}

In this case, the length of the variable that is passed is 64 bits. If you only need the last 32
bits of this variable to be passed into the npi_fsdbw_add_attr() method, it is recommended
to define a size determined function before calling any attribute-adding method like
npi_fsdbw_add_attr().
In the following pseudo code, a new function named as trans_add_attr() is defined which
determines the size of the variable that must be passed to the npi_fsdbw_trans_add_attr()
method before calling the npi_fsdbw_trans_add_attr() method. Using this way, users can
specify a specific range of variable to be passed as an argument into any attribute-adding
methods.
bool trans_add_attr(npiFsdbwTransHandle trans, const char* attrName,
NPI_INT64 localVar, int need_length){
if( need_length <=8 ){
NPI_BYTE8 var = (NPI_BYTE8) localVar;
return npi_fsdbw_trans_add_attr(trans, attrName, localVar);
}
else if( need_length >=8 && need_length <=16 ){
NPI_INT16 var = (NPI_INT16) localVar;
return npi_fsdbw_trans_add_attr(trans, attrName, localVar);
}
else if( need_length >=16 && need_length <=32 ){
NPI_INT32 var = (NPI_INT32) localVar;
return npi_fsdbw_trans_add_attr(trans, attrName, localVar);
}
NPI_INT64 var = (NPI_INT64) localVar;
return npi_fsdbw_trans_add_attr(trans, attrName, localVar);
}
}
void user_define_function( npiFsdbwTransHandle trans, NPI_INT64 var)
{
truncate_head( var );
trans_add_attr(trans, "truncated_attr", var, 32);
}

