Using Wildcard Characters
You can use the asterisk (*) and question mark (?) wildcard characters in -node and
+node entries. For example, consider the following code snippet for Verilog and VHDL
signals:
-module test
+module param_dut
+node test.w*
+node test.logic?
-node VHTOP.A*
-node VHTOP.B?
The asterisk(*) can represent multiple characters, while the question mark (?) represents a
single character.
Excluding Interface Signals From a Design
To exclude interface signals from a given design, use the following syntax:
-node <instance pattern> <signal pattern>
An example to exclude interface signals from a Verilog design is as follows:
interface intf(input bit clk);
logic dvld;
logic ack;
endinterface
module mod(intf intf1, input clk);
always @(posedge clk)
$display("\n Hello World ");

module tb();
logic clock;
intf intf_0(clock);

mod mod_1(intf_0,clock);
mod mod_2(intf_0,clock);
clock = 0;

forever #3 clock = ~clock;
end
#15 $finish;
You can use the following command to exclude the ack signal from all instances of the mod
module:
-node tb.intf_*.ack
You can exclude all the members of a structure using the same syntax as that
for objects in an interface. For example, consider the following code snippet:
typedef struct
{
reg a;
reg b;
}struct1;
struct1 st1;
reg c;
endmodule;
module tb();
top t1();
endmodule;
To exclude only the a member of st1, use the following line in your -cm_hier
file:
-node tb.t1 st1.a
To exclude all the members of st1, use the following line in your -cm_hier file:
-node tb.t1 st1.*
Excluding Driverless Signals for Toggle Coverage
Driverless signals are signals that have nothing in the design that can assign a value to
them. VCS supports automatically detecting driverless signals and excluding them from
coverage monitoring.
To direct VCS to automatically detect and exclude driverless signals, use the
-cm_driverlessnoconst
-
cm_driverlessnoconst switch with the -cm_seqnoconst switch at compile time, as
vcs test.v -cm line+cond+tgl -cm_seqnoconst -cm_driverlessnoconst

1) This feature will be made default as part of the -cm_seqnoconst switch
in a future release. 2) The -cm_driverlessnoconst switch works when the
-cm_seqnoconst switch is also given on the command line.
Consider the following example that shows driverless signals:
Example 15 Driverless Signals
module bot(sig1, sig2, sig3, clk, out1, out2);
input logic sig1;
input logic sig2;
inout sig3;
input clk;
output reg out1;
output reg out2;
logic [1:0] local_signal;

always @(posedge clk) begin
out1 <= sig1 && sig2;
end

reg r1, r2;
reg clk;
wire r3, o1, o2;
initial clk = 0;
always #5 clk = ~clk;
bot b1 (.sig1(r1), .sig2(r2), .sig3(r3), .clk(clk),
.out1(o1), .out2(o2));
#50; $finish();
end
In the example, sig1 and sig2 signals are not driven by any value. They are tied to r1
and r2 in the parent module, respectively, but neither of those signals has any way to
be assigned a value. As the sig1 signal is assigned to the out1 signal, out1 becomes a
driverless signal.

Thus in the example, when the -cm_driverlessnoconst switch is used at compile time,
sig1, sig2, and out1 signals are not considered for coverage monitoring.
The out2 signal is an undriven output and the local_signal signal is an undriven local
signal. When -cm_driverlessnoconst is used, both out2 and local_signal signals are
excluded from coverage computation.
Note that a VDB created without using the -cm_driverlessnoconst switch can be
merged with a VDB created using this switch. However, if a base VDB is created without
using this switch and an input VDB is created using this switch, driverless signals of their
merged VDB are not marked as unreachable.
Figure 3 shows that sig1, sig2, sig3, out1, out2, and local_signal are not considered
for coverage monitoring and therefore, are marked as unreachable in the report. In
Example 15, only clk is considered for coverage monitoring, as clk is not a driverless or
an unreachable signal.
