Hierarchical Cross Coverage
SystemVerilog supports the covergroup construct to capture the functional coverage
model. One of the requirements for a cross is that all the crossed elements must be
declared within the same covergroup. This allows seamless and unambiguous sampling,
among other benefits.
However, advanced coverage modeling requires the ability to specify crosses of elements
in different covergroups. This is called hierarchical covergroup features. The VCS
SystemVerilog implementation has extended the IEEE 1800 standard to enable this
feature.
You can define crosses between coverpoints that reside in separate covergroup instances.
This enables object composition-like behavior for covergroups, in which an instance
of a covergroup can make use of the coverage collected by its constituent covergroup
instances.
Example 33 shows a hierarchical covergroup, hier_cg.
Example 33 Hierarchical Covergroup, hier_cg
class cl_1;

covergroup cg_0 @(e0);
cp0 : coverpoint a;
endgroup : cg_0

covergroup cg_1 @(e1);
cp1 : coverpoint b;
endgroup : cg_1

covergroup hier_cg;
cr_0 : cross cg_0.cp0, cg_1.cp1;
// Using implicit class member

// covergroup instances: cg_0, cg_1
endgroup : hier_cg
endclass: cl_1
Here, the hier_cg covergroup crosses coverpoints, cg_0.a and cg_1.b. The hier_cg
instance is thus a hierarchical covergroup instance with cg_0 and cg_1 as its constituent
instances.
The covergroups, which contain cross of coverpoints from other covergroups, cannot have
a declaration of another coverpoint.
covergroup hier_cg;
cp3 : coverpoint c; // <--- Declaring cp3 is not allowed
cr_0 : cross cg_0.cp0, cg_1.cp1;
endgroup : hier_cg
Constituent Covergroup Instances
A hierarchical covergroup definition can use any covergroup instance visible in its scope
to define crosses. Alternatives to using implicit class member instances are shown in the
following sections:
• Referring to Covergroups Using Cross Module Referencing
• Passing Constituent Instances as Arguments
Referring to Covergroups Using Cross Module Referencing
In Example 34, cg1_inst and cg_2_inst are visible only inside the initial block, named
blk1. The hier_cg hierarchical covergroup refers to them via XMR (Cross Module
Referencing):
Example 34 Hierarchical Covergroup via XMR
module hier_cg_ex;
event cov_e1, cov_e2;
bit [5:0] a, b;

covergroup cg_1 @(cov_e1);
cp0: coverpoint a;
endgroup: cg_1
covergroup cg_2 @(cov_e2);
cp1: coverpoint b;
endgroup: cg_2

covergroup hier_cg;
cr_1 : cross blk1.cg_1_inst.cp0, blk1.cg_2_inst.cp1;
endgroup: hier_cg

initial begin: blk1
cg_1 cg_1_inst = new();
cg_2 cg_2_inst = new();
hier_cg h_cg_inst = new();
end: blk1

endmodule: hier_cg_ex
Passing Constituent Instances as Arguments
It is possible to define a covergroup with constituent instances passed as arguments at the
time of its instantiation, as shown in Example 35.
Example 35 Constituent Instances as Arguments
covergroup cg_0;
cp0 : coverpoint p;
endgroup: cg_0

covergroup cg_1;
cp1 : coverpoint q;
endgroup: cg_1

