Sampling Hierarchical Covergroups
A hierarchical covergroup infers the sampling event from its constituents and cannot have
its own sampling event.
A hierarchical covergroup is considered sampled at a given time stamp if and only
if all of its constituent covergroups are sampled at that time stamp. If one or more of
the constituent covergroups did not get sampled, the results are not collected for the
hierarchical covergroup. The cross hit of a hierarchical cross is composed of the values
recorded for the respective coverpoints. An interesting situation occurs when, at a given
time stamp, one or more constituent covergroups get sampled more than once. This is
illustrated for Example 36 in Setting Up Coverage Metrics.
Example 36 Constituent Covergroups Sampled More Than Once
class cl_0;
covergroup cg_0 @(e0);
cp0 : coverpoint p;

endgroup : cg_0

covergroup cg_1 @(e1);
cp1 : coverpoint q;
endgroup : cg_1

covergroup hier_cg;
cr_0 : cross cg_0.cp0, cg_1.cp1;
endgroup : hier_cg
endclass : cl_0

function new();
cg_0 = new();
cg_1 = new();
hier_cg = new();

