Controlling Measure Merging in Excel Export
When you export an HVP plan to Excel XML hvp genxls, measures with the same metric
types may be merged into shared columns by default.
• -merge_measures 1 (default): Merge measures using the same metrics into shared
columns.
• -merge_measures 0 : Keep each measure as a separate entry with its own score
columns, even if multiple measures use the same metric types (for example, Group).
Use this when you need to display separate coverage scores for each measure in the
generated Excel plan.
Subplan Declaration
A plan or feature can contain one or more subplans as if they contain child features. To
add a plan under another plan or feature, you need to use the subplan keyword with one
mandatory plan name identifier as illustrated in the following example.

You need to first declare a plan identifier where the subplan resides (see
Plan Declaration), otherwise Verification Planner-enabled applications (see
Verification Planner-Enabled Applications) issue an error message of “unknown
plan”. After you add the subplan, use the subplan name identifier to access
the subplan. For more information regarding the naming coventions, refer to
Naming of HVP Plan Items
The BNF for subplan is as follows.
subplan-declaration ::=
subplan plan-identifier[#(subplan-parameters)];
subplan-parameters ::= attribute-value-specification
{,attribute-value-specification}
attribute-value-specification ::= attribute-identifier
= attribute-value-literal
attribute-identifier ::= identifier
In the following example, cache_plan is a subplan of cpu_plan:
plan cache_plan;
attribute string root_mod = "";
feature cache1;
measure Line cov;
source = "instance: ${root_mod}level2"

plan cpu_plan;
subplan cache_plan #(root_mod="top.");

One set of verification plan files may have multiple plan definitions (each plan definition is
encapsulated in a planendplan block). However, all plans must be used as subplans
except for the last plan definition. Only the last plan can be the top-level plan. In the
following example, only plan C is the top-level plan, while plan A is a subplan of plan B
and plan B is a subplan of plan C.
plan A
plan B ( subplan A)
plan C ( subplan B ) //plan C is the top-level plan
If you specify several plans without declaring them as subplans, this results in more than
one top-level plan and Verification Planner-enabled applications issue an error message.
In the following example, none of the plans are declared as a subplan, therefore plan A, B,
and C are all top-level plans. This results in an error condition.

plan A
plan B
plan C //Error, plan A, B, C are all top-level plans
If you specify several plans without declaring them as subplans, only the last plan is
processed as top-level plan and the rest of them are ignored. In the following example
code snippet, plans A and B are ignored, and plan C is the top-level plan.
plan A // ignored
plan B // ignored
plan C // top-level plan

