Tunneling SPICE Supply Ports Through UPF Supply Network
Mixed signal simulation with VCS AMS allows tunneling through Verilog. That is, several
ports of analog instances which are connected through Verilog are considered as
connected inside analog, and do not get A2D/D2A interface elements inserted.
You can use the -power=ams_tunnel_thru_upf option at compile time to enable
tunneling of SPICE supply ports through UPF supply network. That is, when several
SPICE supply ports are connected to the same UPF supply net (in the same hierarchy or
different hierarchies), the SPICE supply ports are considered as connected inside analog,
and do not get the A2D/D2A interface elements inserted. This tunneling mechanism
replaces the connection with A2D/D2A interface elements by a direct connection inside
analog database.
% vcs -power=ams_tunnel_thru_upf -ad=vcsAD.init -upf <upf_file.upf>
-sverilog <design files>
This feature improves the SPICE simulation accuracy and performance by creating a
single D2A or A2D interface element to communicate the supply value between VCS and
XA.
The following sections describe this feature in detail:
SPICE Supply Port Driving a UPF Supply Net
If a SPICE supply port (for example, P1) drives a UPF supply net, and if the same supply
net drives another SPICE supply port (for example, P2), then these two SPICE ports
(P1 and P2) are tunneled and a single UPF-A2D interface element is created (instead of
creating both UPF-A2D and UPF-D2A elements) as shown in Figure 92.

Tunneling SPICE Supply Ports Through UPF Supply Network
Figure 92 SPICE Supply Port Driving a UPF Supply Net
UPF Supply Net Driving Multiple SPICE Supply Ports
If a UPF supply net drives multiple SPICE supply ports, then all those SPICE ports are
tunneled and a single UPF-D2A interface element is created (instead of creating separate
UPF-D2A elements for each of these SPICE input ports) as shown in Figure 93.

Tunneling SPICE Supply Ports Through UPF Supply Network
Figure 93 UPF Supply Net Driving Multiple SPICE Supply Ports
Multiple SPICE Supply Ports Driving the Same UPF Supply Net
If multiple SPICE supply ports drive the same UPF supply net, then all those SPICE ports
are tunneled and a single UPF-A2D interface element is created (instead of creating
separate UPF-A2D elements for each of these SPICE output ports) as shown in Figure 94.

Creating Custom VCTs for Real Data Types
Figure 94 Multiple SPICE Supply Ports Driving the Same Supply Net
Following is the limitation:
• If a UPF supply net is connected to a SPICE inout port, then the inout port is treated
either as an input port or as an output port.
Creating Custom VCTs for Real Data Types
VCS NLP supports creating custom VCTs for real data types, including wreal and
real-valued SystemVerilog nettype HDL types. You can use the -function option of
the create_hdl2upf_vct and create_upf2hdl_vct commands to perform value
conversions between real data type and UPF::supply_net_type.
The following is the use model to create custom VCTs for real data types:
• Use model for the create_upf2hdl_vct command:
create_upf2hdl_vct vct_name
-hdl_type {<sv> [typename]}
-function { package::function_name }

Creating Custom VCTs for Real Data Types
Where, typename is the real value and function_name is the user-defined function.
The function must have only one input of type UPF::supply_net_type and its return
value must be of type real.
create_upf2hdl_vct upf2hdl -hdl_type {sv REAL} -function pkg::func
For example, func is defined as follows:
package pkg
function real func(input UPF::supply_net_type inp);
<conversion logic>
endpackage
